# Minimum statement coverage per package, enforced by
# scripts/check-coverage-floors.sh (`make cover-floors`).
#
# A ratchet, not a target. Raising a floor is a normal part of adding tests.
# Lowering one is a deliberate act, and the point of keeping them here is
# that it shows up in review as an edit to this file rather than as a number
# quietly sliding on a dashboard.
#
# Why per package rather than one number for the module: the module total
# moves for reasons unrelated to the package someone just changed, so a
# well-tested new package can mask a regression elsewhere. client/cmd fell
# from 88.7% to 76.6% over a fortnight while the module total barely moved.
# That is the failure this file exists to catch.
#
# Floors are set at the current value less a small margin, so ordinary churn
# does not trip them but a real regression does. Packages with no tests at
# all are listed at 0 rather than omitted: an entry that has to be edited to
# stay honest is better than silence.

